# Project memory — CERs and ADRs

Memory is **bidirectional**: look back before coding, write forward when the
change lands. Stale records are as bad as ignored ones.

| Kind | Path | Level |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **CER** (code evolution) | `docs/evolution/` | Symbol / pre-post behavior |
| **ADR** (architecture) | `docs/adr/` | System boundaries and contracts |

Indexes: `docs/evolution/README.md`, `docs/adr/README.md`.

## Loop (every non-trivial change)

### 1. Look back

- Skim indexes for records touching the area (security, deps/locks, IDE,
  parse/import/pipeline, publish, perf).
- Read matching CERs/ADRs; treat **post-behavior** / **Decision** as the baseline.
- Do not reintroduce documented pre-behavior unless the user asks to reverse it.

### 2. Change the code

- Prefer extending post-behavior over “simplifying back” to pre-behavior.
- Perf: measure first (ADR-003). Security: fail closed at boundaries (ADR-001).

### 3. Write forward (same change set — not a later chore)

Update or add records **before calling the work done**:

| What happened | What to write |
| --- | --- |
| New lasting code-level fix / behavior shift | New CER entry or new CER; refresh index |
| New or changed system boundary / contract | New ADR or amend Accepted ADR; refresh index |
| Reverses a prior decision | Mark old record `Superseded by …`, link new one |
| Extends an existing decision without reversing it | Amend the living CER/ADR (post-behavior, consequences, commits) |
| Pure typo / comment / docs unrelated to decisions | No CER/ADR required |

Ship code + memory together. A PR/commit that changes a decided behavior without
touching the matching CER/ADR is incomplete.

## Anti-devolution examples (unless a new record supersedes them)

- IDE helpers: `python -I`, bundled path, no workspace `PYTHONPATH`
- Analysis/transpile: no pip-install / no third-party import for typing by default
- `PYS_WORKSPACE_ROOT` → realpath containment; no `interpreter.path` in `pys.deps`
- Hashed fail-closed locks; no second `tokenize` in `compile_pys`
- `run_source` tests for dep-free examples under this repo: set
  `PYS_WORKSPACE_ROOT` to the example’s directory (CER-001 §4) — never inherit
  the root MySQL `pys.lock` (breaks Linux CI with win-amd64 platform mismatch)
- Keep parse memoization; do not re-`resolve()` already-resolved package parents without measurement + CER
- Do not force PEG (`_BRACE_ENGINE = "peg"`) without a CER showing packrat ≤ RD on the gate corpus
- Keep `parse_program_from_tokens` / single-lex compile path; do not restore double-counted phase benches
- Structs stay identity-free value types (ADR-005 / CER-004): no methods/inherits/`new`; fields always public (visibility on the struct); copy on assign/call/return; hash only when all fields fix / `fix struct`
- `data` / `entity` stay separate from `struct` (ADR-011 / CER-011): `data` = frozen VO (all-fields equality); `entity` = identity keys only (`identity(...)` fix); no `uses` on either; do not fold entity identity into `struct`
- Lambdas (ADR-012 / CER-012): capture by value at creation; read-only unless `shared` or `atomic`; foreach loop vars stay immutable per iteration; do not restore Python late-binding
- Atomic (ADR-013 / CER-013): `atomic <primitive> name = expr` implies shared for capture; indivisible `+=`/`-=`/`++`/`--` / `get` / `compareAndSet`; reject `*=`/`/=`/`%=` and redundant `shared`; keep `_PysShared` unchanged; do not add memory-order knobs or float atomics without a new record
- Debug stepping (ADR-014 / CER-014): launch generated program under debugpy (not `transpiler run` subprocess); emit `.pysmap.json` line maps + capture `names`; remap breakpoints (in+out), stack, Variables; halt at breakpoints only (`stopOnEntry: false`); Clear All Breakpoints UX; `prepare_debug` PYTHONPATH = temp + `pys.deps` sites (parity with Run); do not weaken ADR-001; no scalar unwrap of shared/atomic; tasks/threads still deferred
- Enums stay nominal closed sets (ADR-006 / CER-005): all-or-nothing values; no `@alias` / match until a new record; casing is Warning + IDE quick fix, not a hard error
- Abstract classes (ADR-010 / CER-009): `sealed` XOR `abstract`; abstract methods
  only in abstract classes (no body); nominal types; emit `ABC`/`@abstractmethod`
  (Python only — no `@` in PYS source); reject direct instantiate
- Interface methods (CER-010): no access modifier on signatures (always
  public/abstract); reject `public`/`private`/… inside `interface` bodies;
  implementing class methods still need `public`
- **No `@` annotations / decorators in PYS.** An `@foo` mark usually means the
  need was not designed as a language construct. Prefer a real keyword, form,
  or declaration (e.g. enum aliases → proper syntax later, not `@alias`).
  Do not grow a decorator surface to paper over missing constructs.
- Int literals / bitwise (ADR-007 / CER-006): `0b`/`0x`/`_`; width aliases are
  unsigned ranges; `and`/`or`/`not` stay logical; rotate deferred
  ([F-001](../../docs/TODO-FUTURE.md#f-001-bitwise-rotate) in `docs/TODO-FUTURE.md`)
